  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Of the $7$ students who are going to take a test, the top $2$ will get different prizes. In how many ways can both prizes be awarded?",
  "worked_solution": "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ students, so $n=7.$ We want to select $2$ of those students for both the prizes, so we have $r=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the prizes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{P}_{2} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
